{
  "action": "In general, a standard system update will make all the necessary changes.",
  "cves": [
    "CVE-2020-22217",
    "CVE-2026-69184",
    "CVE-2026-69186",
    "CVE-2022-4904"
  ],
  "description": "It was discovered that c-ares incorrectly parsed certain SOA replies. A\nremote attacker could possibly use this issue to cause c-ares to crash,\nresulting in a denial of service. This issue only affected Ubuntu 16.04 LTS\nand Ubuntu 18.04 LTS. (CVE-2020-22217)\n\nIt was discovered that c-ares incorrectly handled certain sortlist strings.\nA remote attacker could possibly use this issue to cause c-ares to crash,\nresulting in a denial of service, or execute arbitrary code. This issue\nonly affected Ubuntu 16.04 LTS. (CVE-2022-4904)\n\nIt was discovered that c-ares did not properly limit DNS compression\npointer hops or assembled name lengths when parsing DNS names. A remote\nattacker could possibly use this issue to cause c-ares to use excessive\nresources, resulting in a denial of service. (CVE-2026-69184)\n\nIt was discovered that c-ares did not properly validate record count fields\nbefore allocating memory when parsing DNS responses. A remote attacker\ncould possibly use this issue to cause c-ares to use excessive resources,\nresulting in a denial of service. This issue only affected Ubuntu 24.04 LTS\nand Ubuntu 26.04 LTS. (CVE-2026-69186)",
  "id": "USN-8951-1",
  "isummary": "Several security issues were fixed in c-ares.",
